  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
